---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { propagation, meta } from "@/lib/load";
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/paths";
import { FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL, REQUIREMENTS } from "@/lib/profile-labels";
import type { FieldSource } from "@/lib/profile-labels";

const sources: FieldSource[] = ["standard", "policy", "ai-plan", "tracker"];

const corpusStart = meta.corpusStart ? formatDate(meta.corpusStart) : "November 2025";

const tierCounts: Record<"added" | "present-at-start" | "tied", number> = {
  added: 0,
  "present-at-start": 0,
  tied: 0,
};
for (const c of propagation.clusters) {
  const t = c.firstObserved?.tier;
  if (t) tierCounts[t] += 1;
}
---

<Layout
  title="Reading the data"
  description="The anatomy of a change on this site (how much the text changed, and which answers changed), and how to read the adoption charts, originality figures and reuse heat-map."
>
  <div class="page reading prose stack">
    <h1>Reading the data</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most Commonwealth agencies must publish a statement about how they use AI.
      This site keeps every version of those statements and reads each change. The
      anatomy of a change, below, is the place to start: the timeline, the profiles
      and the adoption charts are all built from it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy of a change</h2>
    <p>
      Every night the tracker fetches each agency&rsquo;s statement. When the text
      differs from the last good capture, the new capture is a <em>change</em>.
      Before anything else reads it, a language model checks that the capture is
      the statement at all, and not a firewall&rsquo;s block page, an index page
      that only links to the statement, or a page that failed to load part-way.
      Captures that fail the check are left out. Each change that passes is read
      twice: once for how much the text changed, and once for which of the
      statement&rsquo;s answers changed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both readings record what a statement <em>says</em>. A statement that
      doesn&rsquo;t mention a use-case register may come from an agency that has
      one, and a statement that claims one is no proof of it. None of the figures
      on this site is a finding that an agency has or hasn&rsquo;t done something.
    </p>

    <h3 id="changes">How much the text changed</h3>
    <p>
      Every change falls into one of four tiers. Rules settle the mechanical cases
      (formatting, links, page furniture, a date stamp) and the model reads the
      rest.
    </p>
    <dl class="tiers">
      <dt>Noise</dt>
      <dd>
        Nothing the agency wrote changed: a sidebar rotated, a &ldquo;last reviewed
        3 days ago&rdquo; counter ticked over, or the page was rebuilt around the
        same words.
      </dd>
      <dt>Cosmetic</dt>
      <dd>The agency edited wording, punctuation, formatting or a link, and nothing of substance.</dd>
      <dt>Reworded</dt>
      <dd>
        The statement reads differently, rearranged or said at more length, but
        claims and commits to the same things.
      </dd>
      <dt>Substantive</dt>
      <dd>A claim, commitment, fact or disclosure was added, removed or altered.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Substantive and reworded changes appear on the timeline and in each
      statement&rsquo;s story, each with a one-sentence summary the model writes
      from the diff and a list of the points of substance it found, removals
      included. Wherever the site counts &ldquo;changes of substance&rdquo;, it
      counts the substantive tier alone. Noise and cosmetic changes are hidden
      unless you ask for them, and the rare change nothing has read yet shows as
      not yet read.
    </p>

    <h3 id="profiles">Which answers changed</h3>
    <p>
      The model also reads every version of every statement into a <em>profile</em>:
      a fixed set of questions, each with a fixed set of answers.
      Most of the questions come from the instruments behind the statements. The
      DTA&rsquo;s Standard sets eight things every statement must contain, policy
      v2.0 adds five requirements of its own, and the APS AI Plan asks each agency
      for a Chief AI Officer. The rest are ours, and are marked as ours.
    </p>
    <p>
      Comparing one version&rsquo;s profile with the one before gives the second
      reading of a change: the answers it gained, dropped or altered, each filed
      under the question it answers. &ldquo;Chief AI Officer: planned &rarr; in
      place&rdquo; is one such answer; &ldquo;Commitment dropped (will not):
      &hellip;&rdquo; is another. A bullet point grown into a paragraph about the
      same thing changes no answer. Profiles record job titles but never
      people&rsquo;s names.
    </p>
    <h4 id="schema">Where the questions come from</h4>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/whos-who")}>who&rsquo;s who</a> page explains how the
      three instruments fit together. A question is a requirement only if its source
      makes it one; the Chief AI Officer, in particular, comes from the AI Plan and
      not the policy. The usage patterns and domains are the DTA&rsquo;s own
      classification, word for word.
    </p>
    <dl class="sources">
      {
        sources.map((source) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].href ? (
                <a href={FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].href}>{FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].long}</a>
              ) : (
                FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].long
              )}
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <ul>
                {REQUIREMENTS.filter((q) => q.source === source).map((q) => (
                  <li>{q.question}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h3 id="checking">Checking a reading</h3>
    <p>
      Both readings are a model&rsquo;s, and either can be wrong. When an agency
      rewrites a section, a commitment it has only reworded can show as dropped
      alongside its new wording as added. Most of the dropped commitments listed on
      this site come from such rewrites, and some are genuine drops; the diff beside
      each one tells them apart. A vaguely described role can also land in the wrong
      category. Each version is read
      against the previous version&rsquo;s profile to limit that drift. Every
      summary names the model that wrote it and sits beside the diff, and every
      profile sits beside the statement it came from, so you can check one against
      the other. The <a href={withBase("/data/")}>downloads</a> carry the detail
      the site leaves out: the finer kinds within each tier, whether a rule or the
      model decided, and every profile field for every version.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corpus">When the record starts</h2>
    <p>
      Tracking began on {corpusStart}, the week the Australian Public Service
      launched its AI Plan (the <a href={withBase("/about")}>About page</a> tells
      the story). The statements themselves have been <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >required since February 2025</a
      >, so their first nine months, when agencies were drawing their wording from
      one another, came before the tracker started. Every statement that already
      existed enters the record on day one, marked &ldquo;first tracked&rdquo;,
      because we don&rsquo;t know when it was published or what it looked like
      before then. That gap limits much of the analysis below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="adoption">Adoption over time</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/policy")}>what the statements say</a> page counts,
      for each month since tracking began, how many tracked statements&rsquo;
      profiles report each item (a Chief AI Officer, a register, mandatory
      training, a reference to version 2.0), as a share of the statements tracked
      that month. Early months have small denominators: {meta.counts.statements}{" "}
      statements are tracked now, and fewer were on day one. Agencies also joined
      in waves, so a jump from one month to the next can come from new entrants as
      easily as from new text.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two lists on that page rest on a statement&rsquo;s own dates. A statement
      counts as updated since policy v2.0 when its self-declared last-updated date,
      or a change we observed, falls on or after 15 December 2025. It is listed as
      not updated when we have watched it since before then and seen neither, and
      where we can&rsquo;t tell, we say so. The Standard asks for a review at least
      once a year, so a second list gives the statements whose own last-updated
      date is more than a year old. A statement with no date can be on the first
      list but never the second.
      Both lists change as time passes, so both carry the date they were computed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reuse">Shared passages and the reuse heat-map</h2>
    <p>
      Agencies reuse one another&rsquo;s wording heavily. On each statement page,
      passages carry two different tints. An <em>amber</em> tint means the same
      passage appears in other agencies&rsquo; statements; the deeper the amber, the
      more widely it is shared:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>uncoloured</strong>: unique to that statement;</li>
      <li><strong>a few</strong>: shared with two or three other agencies;</li>
      <li><strong>several</strong>: shared with four to nine;</li>
      <li><strong>many</strong>: shared with ten to twenty-four;</li>
      <li><strong>most</strong>: shared with twenty-five or more.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      An amber passage also carries a small count; open it to see which other
      agencies use the same words, each linked to its statement.
    </p>
    <p>
      A separate <em>cool grey</em> tint marks <em>template wording</em>: phrasing
      taken straight from the Policy or the Digital Transformation Agency&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >template</a
      >, which most agencies start from. Template wording is boilerplate that
      can&rsquo;t be traced to particular agencies, so it gets its own colour and
      has no count to open.
    </p>
    <h3 id="first-observed">First observed in our corpus</h3>
    <p>
      For every shared passage we also record which tracked statement showed it
      earliest, and the order in which the others picked it up. Each passage falls into
      one of three tiers, according to how far that ordering can be trusted:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>added during tracking</strong> ({tierCounts.added}): we watched the
        first agency add the passage well after the record opened, with others
        following later. This is the strongest evidence of order.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>present at the start</strong> ({tierCounts["present-at-start"]}): the
        first agency already had the passage when tracking began, and others adopted it
        later. We can order the latecomers, but not that first agency&rsquo;s own source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>tied</strong> ({tierCounts.tied}): several agencies share the earliest
        date, usually because they were all present on day one, so we make no claim
        about order.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Read this as the order <em>we</em> observed, which is no proof of who wrote a
      passage first. A passage two agencies both carried on day one can&rsquo;t be
      ordered at all, and even a clean ordering says only who we saw with it first.
      The real source could be an agency we don&rsquo;t track, an internal draft
      that never went online, or the DTA template itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="originality">Originality scores</h2>
    <p>
      A statement&rsquo;s originality score is the share of its text, by length, that
      is <em>neither</em> shared with other agencies <em>nor</em> drawn from the
      template. A statement at 80% is mostly in its own words; one at 20% is mostly
      shared or templated language. The leaderboard and the agency grid colour each
      statement on this scale, from borrowed to bespoke.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score measures how much of a statement&rsquo;s text appears elsewhere,
      and it is no grade of effort. Two things lower a score for reasons unrelated
      to effort. Shared text includes wording the Standard
      itself calls for (the document&rsquo;s title, its section headings, the OECD
      definition of an AI system that the policy adopts), so an agency that follows the
      Standard to the letter shares more than one that ignores it. And a statement
      published jointly by two bodies (the AASB and AUASB, for instance) counts as
      shared with itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="more">The fine print</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/about")}>About page</a> has the technical method: how
      scrape glitches are filtered out, how passages are matched, which model does
      the reading, and the limits of the scraping itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because each statement is read from a live web page, a capture sometimes picks
      up the page around the statement as well, and the classifier has to tell the
      two apart. It usually does, but not always, so follow the source link on any
      statement to check the original.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .reading {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .reading h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
  }

  .reading h4 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
  }

  .tiers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
  }

  .tiers dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .tiers dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (width < 34rem) {
    .tiers {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      row-gap: 0;
    }

    .tiers dd {
      margin-block-end: var(--space-2);
    }
  }

  .sources {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .sources dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .sources dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .sources ul {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
